commit a67d63ec63ef8b44d3dcc9c84a9b60b66c5fe2f8
Author: Paul Emsley <pemsley@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Sep 5 15:17:20 2026 +0100

    fix(jats): stop the backfill toasting once per paper
    
    "Fetch JATS full text…" produced an "Ignored an external sidecar
    change" toast for every paper it touched.
    
    sidecar.write is atomic — tmp file plus os.replace — and the watcher
    is built on that: `_do_resync_sidecar` documents that our own writes
    surface as RENAMED, which the RENAMED branch ignores, so a
    CREATED/CHANGED on a `*.alexandria` path means a genuinely external
    writer. That holds for a single unhurried write; it does not hold at
    the write rate of a full backfill. Reproduced from the watcher's own
    event log: alongside the expected
    
        created  …pdf.alexandria.<host>.<pid>.tmp
        renamed  …tmp → …pdf.alexandria
    
    macOS also coalesces the replace into
    
        deleted  s13321-020-00429-4.pdf.alexandria
        created  s13321-020-00429-4.pdf.alexandria
    
    dropping the tmp events entirely. That CREATED is indistinguishable
    from someone dropping a shared sidecar into the library, so the toast
    fired 129 times.
    
    `_on_changed` already consults `_is_suppressed` centrally before
    routing, so the existing mechanism was the right one — the backfill
    simply never called it. jats.backfill takes a `suppress` callable and
    invokes it with both the sidecar and the .jats.xml path before
    writing; _on_jats_backfill passes library_watcher.suppress(path, 60).
    
    Also fixes a guard that had never once run: browse.py tested
    `getattr(self, "watcher", None)` where the attribute is
    `library_watcher`, so the suppression around cached cited-by /
    references writes was dead code.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/alexandria/browse.py b/alexandria/browse.py
